mastery.solution_text:
At $(0,2)$ the field gives $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0$, so the curve has a minimum turning point there and rises on both sides.
$y=\left(x^2+4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}$
$\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\dfrac{1}{2}\left(x^2+4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}(2x)$
$=\dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+4}}$
$=\dfrac{x}{y}$
The curve satisfies the equation at every point, and $y(0)=\sqrt{4}=2$, so it is the solution curve through $(0,2)$.
